Actually let me be measured. The clearest exploit: attacker gets the server to fetch `https://trusted.example.test@evil.com/` and return the body. That confirms SSRF (server makes arbitrary outbound requests, body returned). For internal reach: via redirect from evil.com → `http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/`. fetch follows it (redirect: 'follow'), returns the metadata body to the attacker. This is a full SSRF with response exfiltration, capable of cloud metadata theft. That's CRITICAL.

I'll rate the SSRF as CRITICAL.

